# XBD 4.11 File System Cache
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The section makes the cache invisible: the atomicity, serialization and
# time rules of the rest of the standard hold for what other processes and
# threads see. What reaches the storage and when (the sentences on
# fdatasync(), fsync() and the notes on crashes) cannot be observed without
# restarting the system. File times: XBD.4.12-file-times-update. Atomicity
# of directory operations: XBD.4.4-directory-operations.

assertion  XBD.4.11-file-system-cache.001
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.11
anchor     functions/write.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       A second process sees at once what the first has done: a new
           file and its size, a rename, an unlink, mkdir, rmdir, a symbolic
           link, a hard link (st_nlink 2), data written through another open
           file description, a second write to the same place, a truncation
           and data written by the second process itself; this includes
           names the second process looked up (and did not find) just before.
timeout    120
test       t/001-cross-process-visibility.c

assertion  XBD.4.11-file-system-cache.002
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.11
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.7
class      required
says       A pread() of 256 KiB that runs while another thread keeps
           overwriting the same bytes with pwrite() sees all old or all new
           data, never a mixture, and is never short; UNRESOLVED if the two
           never overlapped.
timeout    120
test       t/002-read-write-atomic.c

assertion  XBD.4.11-file-system-cache.003
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.11
class      untestable
says       Successful fdatasync(), fsync() and aio_fsync() transfer the
           cache entries to storage, in atomic units.
reason     What has reached storage can be seen only after the system stops and restarts without a clean shutdown, which a test running on the system cannot arrange; the return values of the calls are tested in XSH.fsync.
